median, mode, standard deviation, variance, percentiles, skewness,
and kurtosis for available metrics.

Visualization --- use Matplotlib, Seaborn, and Plotly to inspect
distributions, comparisons, and relationships.

📈 Visualizations and Insights

Interpretation note: The insights below describe the patterns
visible in the notebook's saved plots. They are descriptive, not proof
of causation. The charts do not by themselves establish statistical
significance.

1. Scatter Plot --- Likes vs. Impressions

What it shows: Each point represents a post, with impressions on the
x-axis and likes on the y-axis.

Insight: Likes are widely spread across the range of impression
counts. The plot does not show a clear, strong linear relationship
between impressions and likes in this sample. Impressions alone may not
explain why some posts receive more likes than others.

Possible next step: Calculate the correlation and compare likes per
impression across post types and categories.

2. Line Chart --- Daily Engagement Trend

What it shows: The average engagement_score for each posting date,
where engagement_score = likes + comments + shares.

Insight: Daily average engagement fluctuates considerably, with
occasional high and low points. The plotted period does not show an
obvious sustained upward or downward trend.

Possible next step: Aggregate by week or month to reduce day-to-day
noise and check whether recurring patterns exist.

3. Bar Chart --- Number of Posts by Category

What it shows: The number of records/posts in each post_category.

Insight: Post counts are fairly balanced across categories. Fitness
and Technology appear among the higher-count categories, while Fashion
and Food are among the lower-count categories. The differences are
modest rather than extreme.

Business implication: Category-level performance comparisons should
account for the relatively similar sample sizes.

4. Pie Chart --- Gender Distribution

What it shows: The proportion of records by gender category.

Insight: Male, Other, and Female each account for roughly one-third
of the displayed records; the chart also includes a small missing/NaN
segment (about 2%). No single displayed gender category dominates the
distribution.

Data-quality note: Resolve or explicitly retain missing gender
values before reporting audience demographics.

5. Histogram --- Age Distribution

What it shows: The distribution of ages across the records.

Insight: Ages span a broad range, approximately from the mid-teens
to the mid-sixties. The distribution is spread across multiple age
bands, with a visible local peak in the late-thirties range rather than
all records being concentrated in one narrow age group.

Possible next step: Create age bands and compare average engagement
or post counts by age group.

6. Box Plot --- Engagement Rate

What it shows: The distribution of engagement_rate, including its
median, spread, and outliers.

Insight: The distribution is strongly right-skewed: most values are
concentrated near the lower end, while a number of high-value outliers
extend far to the right. A small number of posts therefore have much
higher engagement-rate values than most others.

Possible next step: Check the definition and scale of
engagement_rate, validate extreme values, and use robust statistics
such as the median and interquartile range alongside the mean.

7. Seaborn Count Plot --- Post Type

What it shows: The number of records for each post_type.

Insight: Reel, Video, Text, and Image posts have broadly similar
counts. Reels appear slightly more frequent in the displayed plot, but
the differences between post types are not large.

Important: This chart shows post volume, not which post type
performs best. Compare average engagement rate, likes, shares, or
engagement per impression to evaluate performance.

8. Seaborn Bar Plot --- Average Likes by Category

What it shows: Average likes for each post_category.

Insight: Average likes are relatively close across categories,
generally around the 10,000 mark in the plotted scale. Education,
Travel, Lifestyle, Food, Music, and Technology are toward the higher
end, while Fitness and Fashion appear somewhat lower. The differences
look modest compared with the overall values.

Possible next step: Add confidence intervals or compare medians and
sample sizes before treating small differences as meaningful.

9. Violin Plot --- Follower Count by Sentiment

What it shows: The distribution of follower_count for each
sentiment group.

Insight: The distributions overlap substantially across Neutral,
Negative, and Positive sentiment groups. The plot does not show a clear
separation in follower counts by sentiment. A NaN sentiment group is
also visible, indicating missing sentiment labels are present in the
plotted data.

Data-quality note: Consider handling missing sentiment separately
and compare group medians or sample sizes before drawing stronger
conclusions.

10. Heatmap --- Correlation Matrix

What it shows: Pairwise correlations between numerical columns.

Insight: Most displayed correlations are close to zero, suggesting
weak linear relationships between most pairs of numeric variables in
this dataset. The most noticeable pattern in the plotted matrix is a
negative correlation between impression_count and engagement_rate
(approximately -0.23). The relationship is modest and does not
establish causation.

Caution: user_id and post_id are identifiers, so their
correlations generally should not be interpreted as meaningful business
relationships.

11. Pair Plot --- Likes, Shares, Comments, and Impressions

What it shows: Pairwise scatter plots and individual distributions
for likes, shares, comments, and impressions, colored by post type.

Insight: The scatter panels show broad dispersion and no obvious
strong pairwise linear relationships. The distributions overlap across
post types, so these variables do not clearly separate post types in the
displayed pair plot.

Possible next step: Use a correlation table and a smaller, focused
set of plots to examine specific relationships more clearly.

12. Interactive Plotly Scatter --- Impressions vs. Likes by Post Type

What it shows: An interactive scatter plot of impressions versus
likes, colored by post type. Where configured, marker size represents
shares and hover information shows post category.

Insight: This interactive chart allows individual posts to be
inspected by post type and category. As in the Matplotlib scatter plot,
the broad spread suggests impressions alone do not neatly predict likes.

How to use it: Hover over points to inspect details and compare
clusters across post types. Remember that visual association is not
evidence of causation.

Overall Findings

The dataset contains a wide range of engagement outcomes.

Post counts are fairly evenly distributed across the displayed
categories and post types.

Average likes by category appear relatively similar in the saved
chart.

Engagement rate is right-skewed, with a number of high outliers.

Most numeric correlations are weak; the heatmap shows a modest
negative association between impressions and engagement rate.

The plots are useful for exploratory analysis, but stronger
recommendations require validating metric definitions, handling
missing values consistently, and testing differences between groups.

Data and Interpretation Notes

The notebook's engagement_score is a simple sum of likes,
comments, and shares. It should not be confused with the separate
engagement_rate field.

The saved plots include missing gender/sentiment categories in some
visualizations. Check and document how missing values are handled
before final reporting.

Correlation does not imply causation.

Post volume and post performance are different measures; a category
with more posts does not necessarily have better engagement.

Confirm column names and data-cleaning steps when rerunning the
notebook, especially if the source dataset changes.

